        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01YBNaEb3UN3634DpgHdnY7r",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/concepts/composition-isolation-pattern.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Composition and Isolation Pattern\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path composition together enforce process isolation and a single source of truth for system configuration.\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Composition-Isolation-Pattern\\n  - tech/dotnet\\n  - architecture/security\\n  - architecture/resilience\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly employ the same architectural pattern: centralized injection and resolution, with strict isolation preventing ambient access. They are joint applications of the same principle.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2027-04-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\nBoth [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] enforce the same architectural principle: **centralized composition followed by strict isolation**.\\n\\n**Credential Scoping** handles sensitive configuration: the API key is resolved once at startup by a local secrets loader, then injected into child-process environments in an isolated manner\u2014never leaked into the Hub\u0027s own process environment. A compromised or misbehaving Hub cannot access the credential simply by reading its environment.\\n\\n**Runtime Path Configuration** handles all file-system locations the same way: a single path resolver reads configuration once at startup (from command line, environment variables, config file, or code defaults), validates and auto-creates directories, then reports the effective set to the rest of the codebase. No other part of the system is allowed to rediscover locations from the ambient working directory; everything downstream consumes the already-resolved values.\\n\\n## The Relationship\\n\\nBoth decisions protect system resilience and security by applying the same discipline:\\n\\n1. **Single Composition Point**: Both credential scoping and runtime paths are resolved in exactly one place at startup, never scattered across the codebase.\\n\\n2. **Injection, not Inheritance or Ambient Access**: Credentials are injected into child-process environments; paths are injected into resolved configuration. Neither is inherited from or read directly from process environment or working directory.\\n\\n3. **Isolation**: Because paths are pre-resolved and credentials are scoped to child processes, the risk surface is minimized\u2014a compromised component cannot drift away from the system\u0027s intended topology or gain unintended access to secrets.\\n\\nThis pattern is why adding a new runtime location (such as the Query agent\u0027s instruction directory) or adding a new credential means extending the single composition point, not scattering logic across multiple code paths. It is also why adding a new agent process can follow the same credential-scoping model with confidence\u2014the pattern is already established.\\n\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
