      "assistant_text": "The index and log updates encountered validation errors. However, I\u0027ve successfully created the Synthesis Page itself. Let me answer your question based on what I\u0027ve learned:\n\n---\n\n## The Connection\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are **joint applications of the same architectural pattern**: centralized composition at startup, followed by strict isolation that prevents ambient access.\n\n### Credential Scoping\n[[credential-scoping]] ensures the Anthropic API key is resolved once at startup by a local secrets loader, then injected only into child-process environments. The Hub\u0027s own process environment is never mutated\u2014a compromised Hub cannot leak the credential simply by reading its own environment.\n\n### Runtime Paths\n[[runtime-paths]] handles all file-system locations the same way: a single path resolver reads configuration once at startup, validates it, and then passes the resolved values downstream. No part of the codebase is allowed to rediscover locations from the ambient working directory.\n\n### Why They\u0027re Related\n\nBoth decisions protect resilience and security through the same discipline:\n\n1. **Single Composition Point** \u2014 credentials and paths are both resolved in exactly one place at startup, never scattered across the codebase.\n\n2. **Injection, not Inheritance** \u2014 both are injected into their point of use (child processes for credentials; resolved configuration for paths) rather than inherited from or read directly from ambient environment or working directory.\n\n3. **Isolation** \u2014 because paths are pre-resolved and credentials are scoped, the risk surface is minimized. A compromised component cannot drift from the system\u0027s intended topology or gain unintended access.\n\nThis is why adding a new agent process or runtime location means extending the single composition point, not scattering logic across multiple paths.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/composition-isolation-pattern]].",
